Allow user-mode writes to hardware stack registers in linux-user emulation

PSP/PCSP/USD/SBR are privileged on real hardware: user-space context
switching goes through the kernel (e2k makecontext/swapcontext syscalls),
which qemu-e2k linux-user does not implement (do_swapcontext is a TODO).
Permit the writes in linux-user so the DIRECT coroutine backend can act
as that missing kernel support. Reads were already unprivileged here.

Applies to OpenE2K/qemu-e2k, branch e2k, commit ebc4bbd.

diff --git a/target/e2k/helper_int.c b/target/e2k/helper_int.c
index ff474a0..e7a2acb 100644
--- a/target/e2k/helper_int.c
+++ b/target/e2k/helper_int.c
@@ -243,6 +243,17 @@ void HELPER(state_reg_set)(CPUE2KState *env, uint64_t value, int index)
     case SR_CUIR:
         raise_exception(env, E2K_EXCP_ILLEGAL_OPCODE); /* FIXME */
         break;
+    case SR_PSP_HI:
+    case SR_PSP_LO:
+    case SR_PCSP_HI:
+    case SR_PCSP_LO:
+    case SR_USD_HI:
+    case SR_USD_LO:
+    case SR_SBR:
+        /* Allow user-mode context switching (coroutines, swapcontext):
+         * reads of these registers are already unprivileged, so permit
+         * writes as well in linux-user emulation. */
+        break;
     default:
         if ((env->psr & PSR_PM) == 0) {
             raise_exception(env, E2K_EXCP_PRIV_ACTION);
